---
import { NUMBERS } from "../consts";
---
<section class="bg-paper-2">
  <div class="section container">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal">Measured, not scored</p>
    <h2 class="reveal mt-3 font-display text-4xl leading-tight md:text-5xl">There is no benchmark here.</h2>
    <p class="prose reveal mt-5 text-ink-muted">
      Every tool in this category leads with a score — LongMemEval, LoCoMo, recall@5. None of those measure
      the thing that matters: whether the notes turned out to be worth reading. So here are the only numbers
      I have, from one machine.
    </p>
    <dl class="reveal mt-14 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-14">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-[5rem] leading-none tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[7rem]">{NUMBERS.sessionsRescued}</dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 max-w-[40ch] text-ink-muted"><span class="text-ink">sessions.</span> Claude Code would have pruned these. They now exist nowhere else.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-[5rem] leading-none tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[7rem]">{NUMBERS.transcriptsSeen.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="text-ink-muted"> → </span>{NUMBERS.transcriptsNotes}</dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 max-w-[40ch] text-ink-muted"><span class="text-ink">transcripts, notes.</span> {NUMBERS.transcriptsNoise} were subagent runs, workflow phases, or sidechains — skipped by default. {NUMBERS.transcriptsSeen - NUMBERS.transcriptsNoise - NUMBERS.transcriptsNotes} had nothing durable in them. {NUMBERS.transcriptsNotes} became notes.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="eyebrow reveal mt-12">Author's machine, {NUMBERS.measuredOn}. Yours will differ.</p>
  </div>
</section>
